.TH TASKY 1 "November 2026" "Tasky 0.1.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tasky \- task manager and system monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tasky
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tasky
shows what the computer is doing in real time: processes, CPU, memory, storage
and network.
It can end, suspend, resume or reprioritize processes, watch for resource
alerts, and run CPU, GPU, disk and internet benchmarks.
All monitoring stays on the machine.
The only network use is the internet benchmark, which runs only after you
confirm it.
.PP
Tasky has no command-line options.
Starting it a second time shows the window of the instance that is already
running.
Closing the window keeps Tasky running in the system tray when the desktop
provides one; use the tray menu to quit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GDK_BACKEND
Selects the GTK display backend,
.B wayland
or
.BR x11 .
On GNOME under Wayland, Tasky runs through XWayland when every monitor uses
100% scaling, so that the window position can be remembered.
Setting this variable overrides that choice.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/io.github.mikeholp87.taskmanager/settings.json
Settings.
A settings file that cannot be read is kept as
.I settings.json.invalid
and defaults are used.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/io.github.mikeholp87.taskmanager/
Benchmark history and the
.I logs/
directory.
Command lines and environments are never logged.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/
Login entry created by the "start with the OS" setting.
.PP
The directories above follow
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
and
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
when those are set.
.SH NOTES
Raising a process's priority needs the
.B CAP_SYS_NICE
capability.
Without a system tray host (GNOME needs the AppIndicator extension), closing
the window quits Tasky.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kill (1),
.BR renice (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR ps (1)
.PP
